<header><p class="enc-kicker">Historical factor</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bureaucratic administration</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bureaucratic administration refers to government through trained, salaried officials organised in offices with defined functions, records and procedures, rather than through personal or tribal loyalties alone. It was a recurring basis of imperial strength, allowing states to collect taxes, raise armies and govern large territories over long periods. Notable examples include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scholar-officials">scholar-officials</a>, the ministries of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, the provincial systems of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan">diwans</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>.</p></div>
